    "turn_text": "no, no, let me finish. Look, the province public schools back then were not where they should be. We had made incremental progress, but nowhere near. It's obviously been a decade since then. I think the condition has gotten worse based on that report. But look, this is our collective responsibility. We have to really support bold change, and obviously the mayor and the governor are talking directly on how to best do that with the new commissioner. But there is no greater responsibility that we have than the education of our children, not only preparing them for jobs in the 21st century, but to pursue higher education if that's their decision. And looking at the culture that is described in that report, we all, I think, have the duty not only to acknowledge it, but to commit to doing whatever we can, and certainly as a member of the federal delegation, we will stand ready and do stand ready to support the efforts, whatever the commissioner and the governor and mayor decide to do. But this has been a challenge in urban places like Providence for a very long time. Definitely not unique to Providence. But this report, I think more than anything I've ever seen, is a call to action for all of us.",
