  {
    "episode_id": 91092,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91092.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 66,
    "turn_text": "Well, I mean, look, there's no question. The collective bargaining agreement imposes some limitations on the system. There's no doubt about it. I think we were lucky. We had a union president at the time who was, I think, collaborative with the superintendent. We made some significant changes. But the reality is, There is no question that the success in a school is directly dependent on a great teacher and a great principal. And so you have to have a contract that allows you to support great teachers, to give professional development to emerging great teachers, and to get rid of teachers who aren't getting the job done. And so, you know, you want contracts that provide that opportunity, but you need to support, I mean, you can't build a successful system, in my view, by beating up teachers and principals. because all the research shows that a really excellent teacher and a great principal make all the difference in the world. So we need to have a system that supports and encourages and identifies the great talent and makes certain that people who should be doing other jobs are encouraged to do that in a different profession.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
